---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * Sibling of fitnotes-alternative.astro and strong-alternative.astro. Read the
 * FitNotes header first; the trademark and source-checking rules are identical,
 * and the no-claims-about-their-pricing rule from the Strong page applies here
 * too.
 *
 * Import claims checked against hevy_parser.dart and hevy_importer.dart on
 * 2026-09-05:
 *   - weight unit from the weight_kg / weight_lbs header, distance from
 *     distance_km / distance_miles (hevy_parser.dart:104-111)
 *   - carried: title, exercise_title, reps, duration_seconds, exercise_notes,
 *     set_index, rpe (importer :253), and set_type, which is why warmups
 *     survive as warmups (:197, importer :252)
 *   - real session start/end from start_time / end_time (:90-92)
 *   - superset_id IS parsed (:198) but the importer does NOT rebuild supersets
 *     from it. Do not claim supersets come across. If that changes, this
 *     comment and the page both need updating.
 */

const description =
  'Move to Badger from Hevy and keep the detail. The CSV is the richest of the lot: warmups arrive as warmups, RPE comes with them, and your log stops needing an account.';
---

<HelpLayout title="Moving from Hevy" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Moving to Badger from Hevy"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-05"
    about={['Hevy', 'Workout tracking']}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>Moving to Badger from Hevy</h1>

  <p>
    People usually arrive here wanting the same log without the account behind it, or without paying
    for the parts they use. Hevy is a well-made app and this is not an argument that it is not. It is
    an answer to a narrower question: does your history come with you, and what changes if it does?
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">What the CSV carries across</h2>

  <p>
    Hevy's export is the most detailed of the CSVs Badger reads, so more survives the move here than
    from anywhere else except a FitNotes backup. It is read on your phone, with nothing uploaded.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups stay warmups.</strong> Hevy is the one app in this list whose export marks set
      type, so your ramp-up sets arrive flagged instead of being flattened into working sets and
      quietly polluting your PRs and volume numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every set</strong>, with its date, exercise, weight, reps, distance, and time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your RPE</strong>, carried onto the set rather than dropped.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your exercise notes</strong>, kept with the exercise they belong to, and the workout's
      title as a note on that session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real session times.</strong> Hevy exports a start and an end for each workout, so
      imported sessions show when they ran and for how long.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kilograms or pounds, miles or kilometers</strong>, all read from the file's own column
      names rather than guessed, and stored in the unit you logged in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PRs, recalculated</strong> from the imported history, so your records and graphs start
      out full.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not, so nothing surprises you later:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Supersets, as supersets.</strong> The export marks which sets were grouped and Badger
      reads that column, but it does not rebuild the grouping on import yet. The sets all arrive; the
      pairing does not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your routines.</strong> The export is history, not programs. Badger ships seven
      templates and you can build your own, but the routines are set up again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your folders and categories.</strong> A CSV carries none, so new exercises land in an
      "Imported" category to re-sort. Anything already in your Badger library is matched by name
      rather than duplicated.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>In Hevy, go to <strong>Profile &rarr; Settings &rarr; Export Data &rarr; Export as CSV</strong>.</li>
    <li>
      Get the file onto the phone you train with, however you normally move a file: email it to
      yourself, drop it in cloud storage, use a cable.
    </li>
    <li>In Badger, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from Hevy</strong> and pick the file.</li>
    <li>Check the preview: exercises, workout days, sets, and the detected weight unit.</li>
    <li>Import.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    It runs more than once safely. Sets landing on a date you already have merge into that session,
    and nothing already logged is overwritten. Full detail in
    <a href="/help/getting-started#importing-hevy">importing from Hevy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-you-get">What you get, and what you give up</h2>

  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account.</strong> Your log lives on your phone, so there is nothing to sign into and
      nothing to be locked out of. <a href="/promise">The promise</a> is that written down where you
      can hold us to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing behind a paywall.</strong> No cap on routines, on history, or on graphs, and
      exporting your own numbers is not a paid feature.
      <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription">The no-subscription guide</a> compares what
      free actually means across the trackers people weigh up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gym and machine tracking</strong>, with PRs scoped to the machine you used.
      <a href="/help/gyms">How it works</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deeper per-exercise analysis</strong>: estimated 1RM, eleven graphs per exercise, muscle
      balance and plateau detection. <a href="/help/progress">What it tracks</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What you give up:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The social side.</strong> No feed, no followers, no shared workouts. For a lot of Hevy
      users that is the point of the app, and if it is the thing keeping you consistent then Badger
      is the wrong move. Worth being honest about rather than burying.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloud sync between devices.</strong> Local-first by design. Moving to a new phone is a
      backup file you carry across, which is <a href="/help/phone-migration">a documented path</a>,
      not a background service.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="getting-out">Getting back out again</h2>

  <p>
    Badger exports every set to CSV and backs the whole database up to a
    <a href="/badger-format">documented file format</a> that is ordinary readable text. Neither needs
    an account and neither is a paid feature, so leaving is as easy as arriving.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    Hevy is a trademark of its owner, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by, or
    connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility and nothing else: Badger reads the
    CSV file that their app exports.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
